  "question": {
    "text": "Let <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>C</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math> and <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>C</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math> be two biased coins such that the probabilities of getting head in a single toss are <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mn>2</mn> <mn>3</mn> </mfrac> </mrow> </math> and <math> <mrow> <mfrac> <mn>1</mn> <mn>3</mn> </mfrac> </mrow> </math>,&nbsp;respectively. Suppose <math> <mi>&#945;</mi> </math> is the number of heads that appear when <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>C</mi> <mn>1</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math> is tossed twice, independently, and suppose <math> <mi>&#946;</mi> </math> is the number of heads that appear when <math> <mrow> <msub> <mi>C</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msub> </mrow> </math> is tossed twice, independently. Then the probability that the roots of the quadratic polynomial <math> <mrow> <msup> <mi>x</mi> <mn>2</mn> </msup> <mo>&#8722;</mo><mi>&#945;</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>&#946;</mi></mrow> </math> are real and equal, is",
    "image": null
  },
